  "title": "OpenTelemetry collector pipelines: sampling, batching…",
  "content": "opentelemetry collector looks simple until it has to survive real traffic. Here's the version that skips the happy-path tutorial. The edge cases only show up once real users hit the system.\n\n## The scenario: a collector that works until it doesn't\n\nA team runs a few services on Kubernetes. They deploy the OpenTelemetry Collector as a DaemonSet, point their SDKs at it, and for a while everything is fine. Then traffic grows, someone adds a high-cardinality attribute to a span, and the collector starts dropping data. The dashboards go flat. The on-call engineer restarts the collector, it works for a day, then the same thing happens. The team blames the backend. The backend blames the collector. Nobody looks at the pipeline config.\n\nThe collector is not a magic black box. It is a set of receivers, processors, and exporters wired together in pipelines. Each pipeline has a memory footprint, a CPU cost, and a failure mode. When you put sampling, batching, and routing in the same pipeline without understanding how they interact, you get a config that passes validation but behaves badly under load. This post walks through the mechanics of those three processor types and shows how to build a pipeline that survives a traffic spike without silently discarding the data you need.\n\n## The mechanism: pipelines are queues with processors in the middle\n\nA collector pipeline is defined in the `service.pipelines` section of the config. Each pipeline has a name, a list of receivers, a list of processors, and a list of exporters. The collector creates a fan-out from each receiver to the pipeline, and a fan-in from the processors to each exporter. Data flows as batches of telemetry items (spans, metric data points, log records).\n\nThe important detail is that processors are not free. Each processor runs synchronously in the pipeline goroutine. If a processor blocks, the whole pipeline blocks. If the pipeline blocks, the receiver's queue fills. When the queue is full, the receiver drops data. That drop is counted in the collector's internal metrics (`otelcol_receiver_refused_spans`, `otelcol_receiver_refused_metric_points`, `otelcol_receiver_refused_log_records`), but those metrics are only visible if you are scraping the collector itself.\n\nSo the first failure mode is invisible backpressure. A slow exporter or an expensive processor causes drops at the receiver, and the only symptom is missing data.\n\n### Batching: the memory multiplier\n\nThe `batch` processor groups items into batches before sending them to exporters. It has two knobs: `send_batch_size` (number of items per batch) and `timeout` (maximum time to wait before sending a partial batch). There is also `send_batch_max_size`, which caps the batch size even if `send_batch_size` is larger.\n\nBatching reduces the number of export requests, which matters for backends that charge per request or have rate limits. But it also holds data in memory. If you set `send_batch_size: 10000` and your exporter is slow, the batch processor accumulates up to 10,000 items per batch, and it may hold multiple batches in flight. With spans that carry attributes, 10,000 spans can easily be tens of megabytes. Multiply by the number of pipelines and the number of collector replicas, and you have a memory problem.\n\nA typical mistake is to set a large batch size to reduce cost, then wonder why the collector OOMs during a traffic spike. The batch processor does not have a built-in queue limit; it relies on the exporter to apply backpressure. If the exporter has its own queue (many do), the batch processor can keep filling it until memory runs out.\n\n### Sampling: probabilistic vs. tail-based\n\nThe collector supports sampling at two points. Probabilistic sampling (the `probabilisticsampler` processor) keeps a percentage of spans based on a hash of the trace ID. It is cheap and stateless. Tail-based sampling (the `tail_sampling` processor) buffers spans for a complete trace and makes a decision after the trace is complete, based on policies like latency, error status, or attribute values.\n\nProbabilistic sampling is easy to reason about: if you set `sampling_percentage: 10`, you keep roughly 10% of traces. But it is blind. It will drop the traces that matter (errors, slow requests) at the same rate as the ones that don't. For debugging, that is often unacceptable.\n\nTail-based sampling solves that, but it requires the collector to hold spans in memory until the trace is complete. The `tail_sampling` processor has a `decision_wait` setting (default 30 seconds) and a `num_traces` setting that controls how many traces it tracks. If you have high throughput and a long decision wait, memory usage grows. The processor also needs all spans for a trace to arrive at the same collector instance. In a DaemonSet deployment, spans from a single trace can hit different collector pods, so tail sampling requires a two-tier setup: a local collector that forwards by trace ID to a second layer that does the sampling.\n\n### Routing: connectors and the fan-out problem\n\nThe collector has two ways to route data: exporters with `routing` (via the `routing` processor or the `filter` processor) and connectors. Connectors are pipelines that act as both exporter and receiver. The `routing` connector, for example, sends data to different pipelines based on attributes.\n\nA common pattern is to send all data to one pipeline, then use a `routing` connector to split by service name or environment. The problem is that each downstream pipeline has its own processors and exporters, each with its own memory and CPU cost. If you route to three backends, you triple the export work. If one backend is slow, it can back up the shared pipeline unless you isolate it.\n\nThe collector does not automatically isolate pipelines. A slow exporter in one pipeline can cause the receiver to drop data for all pipelines if they share the same receiver. The fix is to use separate receivers or to put a `memory_limiter` processor at the start of each pipeline to shed load before it reaches the expensive processors.\n\n## The fix: a pipeline that degrades gracefully\n\nThe goal is a pipeline that keeps the data you need under load and sheds the rest in a predictable way. That means:\n\n1. Put a `memory_limiter` processor first in every pipeline.\n2. Use batching with a conservative size and a timeout that matches your backend's latency.\n3. Sample early and cheaply where possible, and use tail sampling only where the cost is justified.\n4. Isolate routing so a slow backend does not take down the whole collector.\n\n### A worked example\n\nAssume a service that produces 20,000 spans per second at peak. Each span averages 1 KB in memory (including attributes). The collector runs as a DaemonSet with 10 pods, so each pod handles 2,000 spans/s.\n\nIf you set `send_batch_size: 10000` and `timeout: 10s`, each pod could hold up to 10,000 spans per batch, which is 10 MB per batch. With multiple batches in flight, memory can exceed 100 MB per pod just for batching. Add the tail sampling buffer (if used) and you are in the hundreds of MB. That is fine for a pod with a 1 GB limit, but it leaves little headroom for spikes.\n\nA more conservative config: `send_batch_size: 2000`, `send_batch_max_size: 3000`, `timeout: 5s`. That caps each batch at 3 MB, and with two batches in flight, batching memory stays under 10 MB per pod. The trade-off is more export requests: at 2,000 spans/s and 2,000 spans per batch, you send one request per second per pod. That is usually acceptable.\n\nFor sampling, a two-tier setup: the DaemonSet collector does probabilistic sampling at 50% (cheap, reduces volume by half), then forwards to a gateway collector that does tail sampling with a 10% keep rate for non-error traces and 100% for errors. The gateway sees 10,000 spans/s, which is manageable for a single instance with enough memory. The `decision_wait` is set to 10 seconds, and `num_traces` is set to 50,000 (roughly 5,000 traces/s * 10s, assuming 10 spans per trace). That requires memory: 50,000 traces * 10 spans * 1 KB = 500 MB. So the gateway needs at least 1 GB of memory, plus headroom.\n\nThis is illustrative arithmetic, not a benchmark. The point is that every knob has a memory cost, and you can calculate it before you deploy.\n\n### Code: a minimal collector config with memory limiter and batching\n\n```yaml\nreceivers:\n  otlp:\n    protocols:\n      grpc:\n        endpoint: 0.0.0.0:4317\n      http:\n        endpoint: 0.0.0.0:4318\n\nprocessors:\n  memory_limiter:\n    check_interval: 1s\n    limit_mib: 400\n    spike_limit_mib: 100\n  batch:\n    send_batch_size: 2000\n    send_batch_max_size: 3000\n    timeout: 5s\n\nexporters:\n  otlp:\n    endpoint: backend.example.com:4317\n    tls:\n      insecure: false\n\nservice:\n  pipelines:\n    traces:\n      receivers: [otlp]\n      processors: [memory_limiter, batch]\n      exporters: [otlp]\n```\n\nThe `memory_limiter` processor checks the collector's memory usage every second. If usage exceeds `limit_mib`, it starts refusing data. The `spike_limit_mib` allows a temporary overshoot. This is the single most effective protection against OOM kills. Place it first so that expensive processors never see data that would push memory over the limit.\n\n### Routing with connectors\n\nSuppose you want to send errors to a different backend for alerting, and everything else to a cheaper storage backend. You can use the `routing` connector:\n\n```yaml\nconnectors:\n  routing:\n    default_pipelines: [traces/cheap]\n    table:\n      - context: span\n        condition: attributes[\"error\"] == true\n        pipelines: [traces/errors]\n\nservice:\n  pipelines:\n    traces/in:\n      receivers: [otlp]\n      processors: [memory_limiter, batch]\n      exporters: [routing]\n    traces/errors:\n      receivers: [routing]\n      processors: [batch]\n      exporters: [otlp/errors]\n    traces/cheap:\n      receivers: [routing]\n      processors: [batch]\n      exporters: [otlp/cheap]\n```\n\nThis config routes spans with an `error` attribute to the errors pipeline and everything else to the cheap pipeline. The `routing` connector evaluates conditions per span. Note that the `batch` processor appears in each downstream pipeline; that is intentional, because batching should happen after routing to avoid sending large batches to the wrong backend. Also, the `memory_limiter` is only in the input pipeline; the downstream pipelines inherit the backpressure but do not have their own memory limiter. If the errors backend is slow, it can still back up the routing connector and eventually the input pipeline. To isolate, you would need separate receivers or a queue.\n\n## What to monitor afterwards\n\nThe collector exposes its own metrics on a Prometheus endpoint (usually port 8888). The metrics that matter:\n\n- `otelcol_receiver_refused_spans` (and the metric/log equivalents): any non-zero value means data is being dropped at the receiver. This is the canary for backpressure.\n- `otelcol_processor_batch_batch_send_size`: the distribution of batch sizes. If it is consistently at the max, your timeout may be too long or your batch size too small.\n- `otelcol_exporter_send_failed_spans`: failed exports. A sudden increase means the backend is rejecting data or the network is failing.\n- `otelcol_processor_memory_limiter_refused_spans`: data refused by the memory limiter. This is expected under load, but a sustained high rate means your limit is too low or your pipeline is too expensive.\n\nSet alerts on `rate(otelcol_receiver_refused_spans[5m]) > 0` for more than a few minutes. That gives you time to react before the data loss becomes significant. Also monitor the collector's own memory usage against its limit; if it is consistently near the limit, you need to either increase the limit or reduce the pipeline cost.\n\n## The broader lesson: pipelines are capacity planning\n\nA collector config is not just a wiring diagram. It is a capacity plan. Every processor adds latency and memory. Every exporter adds a network dependency. The collector does not magically scale; it scales with the resources you give it and the efficiency of your config.\n\nTeams often treat the collector as infrastructure that \"just works\" and only look at it when data is missing. The better approach is to treat the pipeline as code: version it, review it, and load-test it with a synthetic workload that matches your peak traffic. A simple load test with the `telemetrygen` tool (part of the OpenTelemetry project) can reveal backpressure and memory issues before they hit production.\n\nThe principle: design for graceful degradation, not for peak capacity. Assume that at some point the backend will be slow or the traffic will spike. The pipeline should shed load in a controlled way (memory limiter, sampling) rather than crash or silently drop everything.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n### Why does my OpenTelemetry collector drop spans under load?\n\nThe most common cause is backpressure from a slow exporter or an expensive processor. When the pipeline cannot keep up, the receiver's queue fills and it starts refusing data. Check `otelcol_receiver_refused_spans` and `otelcol_exporter_send_failed_spans` to confirm. Adding a `memory_limiter` processor at the start of the pipeline and reducing batch size can help.\n\n### Should I use probabilistic or tail-based sampling in the collector?\n\nProbabilistic sampling is cheap and stateless, but it drops errors and slow traces at the same rate as everything else. Tail-based sampling keeps traces based on policies, but it requires buffering spans and a two-tier collector setup to ensure all spans for a trace reach the same instance. Use probabilistic sampling for high-volume, low-value data and tail sampling for the traces you need for debugging.\n\n### How do I route different telemetry to different backends?\n\nUse the `routing` connector or the `filter` processor to split data based on attributes. Define separate pipelines for each destination and attach the routing connector as an exporter in the source pipeline and as a receiver in the destination pipelines. Be aware that each destination pipeline has its own resource cost, and a slow backend can back up the shared source pipeline unless you isolate it.\n\n### What is a safe batch size for the OpenTelemetry collector?\n\nThere is no universal safe size. Calculate the memory cost: batch size times average item size times number of in-flight batches. For spans averaging 1 KB, a batch size of 2,000 uses about 2 MB per batch. Keep the total batching memory under 10% of the collector's memory limit. Also set `send_batch_max_size` to prevent oversized batches.\n\n## Next step\n\nOpen your collector config and add a `memory_limiter` processor as the first processor in every pipeline. Set `limit_mib` to 80% of the collector's memory limit and `spike_limit_mib` to 10%. Then check the collector's metrics endpoint for `otelcol_receiver_refused_spans` before and after the change. That single edit will not solve every pipeline problem, but it will turn silent data loss into a visible, measurable signal.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [Kubernetes documentation](https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/)\n- [OpenTelemetry documentation](https://opentelemetry.io/docs/)\n- [Prometheus documentation](https://prometheus.io/docs/)\n- [Google SRE Book: Monitoring Distributed Systems](https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
